Source code conventions have mostly been taken from http://fortranwiki.org/fortran/show/Source+conventions,
http://stackoverflow.com/questions/2758635/writing-robust-and-modern-fortran-code,
http://research.metoffice.gov.uk/research/nwp/numerical/fortran90/f90_standards.html and
https://github.com/Fortran-FOSS-Programmers/Best_Practices

This is a short list of things to do and not to do:
- Do not use real*8 and other extensions; Use KINDs from ISO_FORTRAN_ENV (int64 etc)
- Use ISO_FORTRAN_ENV for standard I/O units
- Use NEWUNIT= for writing to files
- Always use implicit none at the start of module, submodule and program (routines inherit it)
- Always use modules; the build system also requires this
- Don't use global variables. If you must, use ALL_CAPS for these
- Don't use gotos, jump labels
- Write modular code
- Use the debug mode
- Write PURE functions as much as possible
- Do not use COMMON blocks
- Do Not use EQUIVALENCE
- Use meaningful variable names, in lowercase, with underscores
- Indent by 2 spaces
- Use spaces to line up = signs
- Write FORD/Doxygen compatible documentation for all public procedures
- Procedures should have a verb in their name

Other remarks:
- Submodules are probably not supported in the build system. Feel free to change this!
